---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Attribution from '../components/site/Attribution.astro';
import { MANUAL } from '../data/citation';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" section="about" description="What C++ Atlas is, who it is for, and how it is built.">
  <div class="wrap-narrow about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>A course in scientific modelling through C++</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        C++ Atlas teaches C++ from the first line of code to the level needed for independent computational research in biology,
        ecology and evolution.
      </p>
    </header>
    <p>
      The goal is not to cover every corner of the language. It is to make a scientist able to read a mathematical model in a paper,
      understand its assumptions, design an algorithm for it, implement that algorithm in C++, verify that the code is correct, run
      simulation experiments, analyse the output and draw conclusions that say no more than the model supports.
    </p>
    <h2>How it is organised</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={url('/atlas/')}>atlas</a> is a map of the curriculum. The foundations of the language lie to the west; the four
      modelling destinations, Modules 14 to 17 of the Groningen course, rise to the east: algorithm design, numerical integration,
      stochastic modelling and individual-based modelling. A final region, scientific inference, is original to this site and connects
      the destinations to experiments, analysis and interpretation.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can travel in both directions: from a construct you have just learned to the models that use it, or from a model you want to
      build back to everything it requires. The <a href={url('/start/')}>start page</a> asks two questions and suggests a route;
      nothing is locked.
    </p>
    <h2>What is honest about the interactive parts</h2>
    <p>
      The laboratories run TypeScript implementations of the models in your browser. They do not compile or execute C++. Every C++
      program on the site is a real file that the project’s continuous integration compiles with GCC and Clang, runs, and compares with
      expected output; the model programs are cross-validated against the browser implementations. The code editor lets you edit a copy
      of a program to take away and compile yourself.
    </p>
    <h2>Curriculum source and attribution</h2>
    <p>
      The curriculum follows <cite>{MANUAL.title}</cite> by {MANUAL.authors.join(', ')} ({MANUAL.institution}).
    </p>
    <div class="key"><Attribution label="Source" /></div>
    <p>
      This is an independent project. It is not an official University of Groningen course and implies no endorsement by the
      University or the authors. Lessons, examples, figures and exercises are written independently, and the manual’s assignments,
      exercise solutions and graded projects are not reproduced. Full details and the section-by-section coverage are on the
      <a href={url('/sources/')}>sources page</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      Colour, typography and scientific figures follow the <a href="https://akaminas.github.io/vellum/">Vellum</a> design system:
      claim titles, direct labels, hairline grids, a fixed series order, and colour that never carries meaning on its own.
    </p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>
      There are no accounts and no tracking. Your level, goals, bookmarks and progress are stored only in your browser; the
      <a href={url('/progress/')}>progress page</a> exports, imports and deletes them.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .about h2 { font-size: 1.6rem; margin-top: 1.8em; }
  .about p { max-width: 68ch; }
</style>
